> Dear colleagues (apologies for cross-posting),
>
> We are inviting expressions of interest to host the International Statistical Ecology Conference (ISEC) in 2026. ISEC is organised every two years (typically in June or July), and it has been a very successful event to build connections across the statistical ecology community all over the world. For information about the last ISEC, held in Cape Town in 2022, please see https://www.isec2022.org/.
>
> ISEC 2026 will be the 10th iteration of the conference, and it has been customary for past organisers to provide some guidance to prospective hosts. Conference hosts can also apply for financial support from the ISEC Foundation.
>
> Please fill out this form to let us know that you are interested in hosting ISEC 2026: https://forms.office.com/r/8nrRGESPPL. We would like to receive applications by November 30, 2023. If you have any questions, or would like to discuss anything, you can contact the chair of the long-term planning committee (Brett McClintock, isec...@gmail.com).
>
> We hope to see many of you in Swansea for ISEC 2024.
>
> Best wishes,
> ISEC long-term planning committee

From uca0b78ecd2f38eec at groups.google.com  Wed Aug 30 17:50:10 2023
From: uca0b78ecd2f38eec at groups.google.com (Josh Barry)
Date: Wed, 30 Aug 2023 17:50:10 +0000
Subject: 85% CRIs using ubms package?
Message-ID: <GGBMSptXeLA.lT9f6tpzBAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/GGBMSptXeLA/m/lT9f6tpzBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear all,

I want to extract the 85% CRIs from a single season occupancy model with package ubms (the Bayesian 'sister package' of unmarked). The model output provides the 95% CRIs. I attempted to use the confint() function which works with unmarked, but it doesn't seem to work with ubms. Does anyone know how I can easily extract the 85% CRIs with ubms?

Unmarked  example:
unmarked_mod_1 <-occu(~ date ~Year , data= data1)
confint(unmarked_mod_1, type="state", level = 0.85)

ubms example (same model but with site-level random effect):
  ubms_mod_2  <-stan_occu(~date ~ Year + (1|Site), data=data1,
                                     chains = 5, iter = 10000, cores = 2, seed = 123)

Thanks for any insights.

Kind regards -- Josh

From u4f80e1927b0757cb at groups.google.com  Wed Aug 30 18:06:42 2023
From: u4f80e1927b0757cb at groups.google.com (Ken Kellner)
Date: Wed, 30 Aug 2023 18:06:42 +0000
Subject: Re: [unmarked] 85% CRIs using ubms package?
Message-ID: <GGBMSptXeLA.SEKP_sF0BAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <GGBMSptXeLA.lT9f6tpzBAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
References: <GGBMSptXeLA.lT9f6tpzBAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/GGBMSptXeLA/m/SEKP_sF0BAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ubms doesn't support the confint method, I should probably add something like that.

Probably the easiest way to get what you want is to just get the full set of posterior samples for all the parameters and calculate the desired CI yourself. For example, to get the state submodel intercept, it would be something like

post <- extract(unmarked_mod_1)
names(post)
beta_state <- post$beta_state
dim(beta_state)
int <- beta_state[,1] # columns in same order as output summary
quantile(int, c(0.075, 0.925))

Ken

